<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<subsection xml:id="subsec-num-project-graphical">
  <title>Graphical Analysis</title>

  <p>
    Explore the numerical variables before performing inference. Use
    summary statistics and graphs to describe the observations, compare
    groups, and assess the conditions for the selected procedures.
    Focus on the observations actually used in each analysis.
  </p>

  <paragraphs xml:id="num-project-summary-tables">
    <title>Required Numerical Summaries</title>
    <p>
      Include a readable summary table containing the sample size, sample
      mean, and sample standard deviation for the one-mean analysis and
      for each independent group used in the two-mean test or ANOVA.
      Identify the response variable and its units.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you choose a paired test, also summarize the paired differences:
      the number of complete pairs, the mean difference, and the standard
      deviation of the differences. State the subtraction order. The
      standard deviation of the differences is calculated from the
      individual differences, rather than by subtracting the two original
      standard deviations.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="num-project-required-graphs">
    <title>Required Graphs</title>
    <p>
      Include histograms and boxplots that support the research questions.
      A graph may support more than one analysis when the relevant
      observations or groups are clearly identified.
      <ul>
        <li><p><alert>One-mean analysis:</alert> Include a histogram of
        the numerical observations used in this analysis. Discuss shape,
        center, variability, and any unusual observations.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>Paired-test option:</alert> Include a histogram of the paired differences. Discuss the shape and unusual
        values in the differences. Plots of the two original measurements
        can provide additional context.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>Independent-test option:</alert> Include two histograms of the two data sets. Include side-by-side
        boxplots comparing the two groups. Discuss differences in center,
        variability, and unusual observations.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>ANOVA:</alert> Include side-by-side boxplots of the
        numerical response for all three or more groups. Examine the
        distributions within groups using histograms or other appropriate
        plots as needed. Discuss group centers, spreads, and outliers.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="num-project-graphic-presentation">
    <title>Presentation and Discussion</title>
    <p>
      Give each table and graph a meaningful title, clear variable and
      group labels, measurement units, and a legend when needed. Use
      readable scales and comparable axes when displaying group
      distributions separately. Include a brief discussion explaining
      what the reader should notice and how it relates to the research
      questions and procedure conditions.
    </p>
    <p>
      Describe potential outliers and check for data-entry or measurement
      issues. Document any exclusions and their scientific or data-quality
      justification. A boxplot alone does not determine whether a
      measurement should be removed.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>
</subsection>
